+Only connection states `active`, `connected`, and `operational` are probed, and only an address inside the VLAN prefix. The vantage address itself is not a target. `can_ping: false` on an IX-F address is an opt-out. When the import is the IXP Manager export, each round also GETs `/admin/api/v4/nagios/customers/{vlanid}/4` or `/6` on that same origin, including a subdirectory install. `vlanid` is the VLAN database id. That is the only extra path. The template suffix and every other nagios route are not called. The same `X-IXP-Manager-API-Key` header is used. The key's user must be a superuser; a 401 leaves that VLAN unprobed. Redirects are not followed. A host is probed only when a service uses `ixp-manager-member-ping-service` (busy: `ixp-manager-member-ping-busy-service`). The busy template uses the looser warning (1000ms or 80% loss; otherwise 250ms or 20% loss). The busy critical RTT (2000ms) is the minimum per-packet timeout for that host. A failed fetch keeps the last successful list. With no successful list, that VLAN is not probed, because the member export omits `can_ping`. A named IX-F URL is not the manager export, so that GET is not made. `GET /api/ixp/probes` serves the round. The metrics are `packeteer_ixp_fabric_up`, `packeteer_ixp_fabric_targets`, `packeteer_ixp_fabric_opt_out`, `packeteer_ixp_fabric_past_tolerance`, and `packeteer_ixp_fabric_age_seconds`. There is no per-member series.
+
+Adding or removing `ixp`, including `members`, `vantages`, and `fabric`, needs a restart. Rollback: remove `vantages` and `fabric`, or the `ixp` block, and restart. Nothing was announced.
 
